course: dictionary
lesson: 7703
title: A psalm is a holy song
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A psalm is a holy song or poem from the Book of Psalms in the Bible. People sing, say or read psalms to praise God, to thank him, or to call out to him for help.
explain: Psalms are sung or said in church and synagogue services, and they are read at weddings and funerals too. Psalm 23 is the best known. It begins «The Lord is my shepherd», which pictures God leading, feeding and protecting, like a shepherd caring for sheep.
explain: The Psalms hold many feelings. Some are full of joy, some are full of fear, some are sad or angry, and some show trust in God. The Psalms can mean the whole book, and a psalm can mean one song from that book.
fact: A psalm is a holy song or poem from the Book of Psalms in the Bible.
fact: «Psalm 23» begins «The Lord is my shepherd» and is often read at funerals to give comfort.
fact: «Psalm 100» is full of joy and thanks.
fact: «Psalm 13» is a sad or angry cry: «How long, O Lord?»
fact: Many psalms are said to come from King David.
exercise: What is a psalm? | *A holy song or poem from the Bible | A kind of fish found in rivers | A type of old coin
exercise: «The Lord is my shepherd» pictures God as a shepherd. What does this picture show? | *God leading, feeding and protecting | A dog chasing a ball | A farmer selling sheep at market
exercise: «Psalm 8 looks up at the moon and stars and wonders why God cares about small human beings.» What is Psalm 8 mostly doing? | *Wondering at the sky and at God's care | Telling a joke | Counting sheep in a field
test: Why is «Psalm 23» often read at funerals? | It makes people laugh | *It gives comfort, because it says God is with us in hard times | It is a recipe for bread | It tells the history of a city
test: The Psalms hold feelings such as joy and trust. Which psalm is full of joy and thanks? | Psalm 46 | *Psalm 100 | Psalm 13
exam: Where are psalms sung or said? | *In church and synagogue services | At the bottom of the sea | In a football stadium | In a school canteen
